WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.166 --> 00:00:01.470
(crowd yelling)

00:00:01.470 --> 00:00:03.960
- [Reporter] The Supreme Court
has overturned Roe V. Wade,

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:07.650
the 1973 decision that
established a constitutional right

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:09.276
to an abortion in the US.

00:00:09.276 --> 00:00:11.328
- [Crowd] Roe V. Wade has got to go!

00:00:11.328 --> 00:00:13.320
- [Crowd] My body, my choice!

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.840
- [Reporter] Now decisions
on the legality of abortion

00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.310
are left to the states.

00:00:17.310 --> 00:00:19.650
- This is going to mean
that access to abortion

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:23.730
will vary dramatically,
depending on where you live.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:26.100
- [Reporter] While 16 states
and the District of Columbia

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:28.440
have laws that protect
the right to an abortion.,

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:32.160
22 states have laws that could
ban all or most abortions

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.990
now that Roe has been reversed,

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:35.730
according to the Guttmacher Institute,

00:00:35.730 --> 00:00:37.770
a policy group that
supports abortion rights

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.890
and tracks national abortion statistics.

00:00:40.890 --> 00:00:43.140
The organization details
a patchwork of laws

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.710
among these states, including
pre-Roe bans, trigger laws,

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.330
and post-Roe restrictions

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.142
that will determine the fate
of abortion access in the US.

00:00:51.142 --> 00:00:52.148
- The resolution passes.

00:00:52.148 --> 00:00:53.943
(gavel bangs)

00:00:53.943 --> 00:00:55.800
- [Reporter] Nine states
have bans on their books

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.480
that predate Roe V. Wade,

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.390
which have been unenforceable
for the past five decades.

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:05.250
- Some of them date back to
the late 1800s or early 1900s.

00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:08.040
These could be laws that could be enforced

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.193
after this decision or as
a result of this decision.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.410
- [Reporter] States like Arkansas

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:15.630
can now revive their Pre-Roe bans

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:19.050
through official enforcement
or by filing court actions.

00:01:19.050 --> 00:01:21.450
However, not all states with Pre-Roe bans

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.170
are poised to enforce the laws.

00:01:23.170 --> 00:01:24.330
(crowd cheers)

00:01:24.330 --> 00:01:25.800
Michigan Governor Gretchen Whitner

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:28.050
and abortion rights
groups have filed lawsuits

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:30.450
to block the state's 1931 law

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:31.830
that makes providing an abortion

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:34.800
at any point during pregnancy a felony.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:37.470
A Michigan judge, through
a preliminary injunction,

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.210
said the state can't enforce it.

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:42.510
- Whereas others in states
like Arizona, for example,

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.670
I know lawmakers there are anticipating

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.330
that they could enforce their
own pre-Roe ban on abortion.

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:50.940
- [Reporter] 13 states have
passed so-called trigger laws

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:52.830
on the condition they would go into effect

00:01:52.830 --> 00:01:54.270
if the Supreme court rescinded

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.340
the constitutional right to abortion.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:01:58.080
- However, each of these
laws are quite different

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.220
from the other one.

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.530
- [Reporter] And they can take
effect at different times.

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:04.230
In Kentucky, Louisiana, and South Dakota,

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:06.510
the laws are enforceable immediately,

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.910
but in Texas, Idaho, and Tennessee,

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:11.940
there's a 30-day buffer
period between Roe's reversal

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.130
and when the laws go into effect.

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.130
In some states, trigger
laws can only be implemented

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:18.960
after state government officials,

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.420
such as Attorneys General or Governors,

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.403
certify the high court's decision.

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:26.790
Some states, including
Georgia and Alabama,

00:02:26.790 --> 00:02:28.410
have sought to set stricter limits

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:32.040
on when abortions can be
performed during a pregnancy.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.360
Georgia's ban on abortion

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.210
around when cardiac
activity can be detected

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.130
and Alabama's near-total ban

00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:40.080
have previously been blocked in court

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.020
because they conflicted
with limits set by Roe.

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.510
- Now the elimination of
thee federal protections

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:47.550
for the procedure and the reversal

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.310
of the Supreme Court precedent
means that it's possible

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.421
they can be resurrected in some way.

00:02:52.421 --> 00:02:54.840
- [Crowd] Save our babies!

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:55.980
- [Reporter] Some states don't have

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.890
these specific restrictions
or protections for abortion

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:01.680
currently on the books
and it remains to be seen

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.140
what actions they will take in the future.

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.780
Meanwhile, abortion clinics
in more liberal states

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:09.330
have been preparing for
an influx of patients.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:12.990
- They are expanding their
hours, adding more staff,

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:15.300
adding more days that they are available

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.920
to accommodate what they expect

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.890
to be even more patients coming in.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.050
- [Reporter] Abortion providers
in more restrictive states

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:24.300
are also assessing how to support patients

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.250
who will need to travel
outside of the state

00:03:26.250 --> 00:03:27.450
for the procedure.

00:03:27.450 --> 00:03:28.620
- Planning and figuring out

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.750
how they will navigate this different

00:03:30.750 --> 00:03:34.110
and varying access to
abortion across this country

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:36.120
will be something that I'm
sure will take some time

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.550
to really nail down and figure out.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:39.767
- [Speaker] What do we do?

00:03:39.767 --> 00:03:40.830
- [Crowd] Hands off, fight back!

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:41.663
- [Speaker] What do we do?

00:03:41.663 --> 00:03:44.056
- [Crowd] Hands off, fight back!

